From 000000000000000000000000000000000000000a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: husky-port CI <ci@husky-port.local>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 10:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] phy-exynos-usbdrd-eusb: link s2mpu at pkvm probe, control via runtime PM

exynos_usbdrd_phy_probe() resolves phy_drd->s2mpu at probe time.  When
the phy wins the probe-order race against pkvm-s2mpu, the link stays
NULL forever and every exynos_usbdrd_s2mpu_manual_control() call
silently no-ops -> S2MPU is never switched to allow-all -> HSI0 SysMMU
PTW access fault at the first USB gadget DMA -> panic.

Fix the race ONLY in exynos_usbdrd_set_s2mpu_pm_ops(), which is called
from pkvm_s2mpu's own probe: at that instant the S2MPU platform device
is guaranteed to be registered, so a phy that probed earlier gets
linked there, deterministically.  Probe context is a safe place for
the device-tree lookup.

Do NOT resolve the link in exynos_usbdrd_s2mpu_manual_control(): that
function runs from the hsi0 power-domain path, where driver-core
lookups (of_find_device_by_node) can deadlock against concurrent device
probes (e.g. dm-node uevents after dmsetup mknodes).  The previous
lazy-lookup variant hard-froze every boot at ~6.1-6.45s on both r8 and
r12 kernels; boots without it always passed that point.

Also replace the raw cross-module function pointer (s2mpu_notify) with
the S2MPU driver's own runtime-PM implementation, as the earlier
s2mpu-runtime-pm-control variant did (the only variant so far that
reached userspace with working USB): the indirect call through a raw
pointer into another module is a CFI/lifetime hazard.
---
diff --git a/soc/gs/drivers/phy/samsung/phy-exynos-usbdrd-eusb.c b/soc/gs/drivers/phy/samsung/phy-exynos-usbdrd-eusb.c
index 80b291b..c1c7701 100644
--- a/soc/gs/drivers/phy/samsung/phy-exynos-usbdrd-eusb.c
+++ b/soc/gs/drivers/phy/samsung/phy-exynos-usbdrd-eusb.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <linux/phy/phy.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
@@ -42,13 +43,38 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(phycon_base_addr);
 
 static int (*s2mpu_notify)(struct device *dev, bool on);
 
+static struct exynos_usbdrd_phy *exynos_usbdrd_get_struct(void);
+
 int exynos_usbdrd_set_s2mpu_pm_ops(int (*cb)(struct device *dev, bool on))
 {
+	struct exynos_usbdrd_phy *phy_drd;
+	struct device_node *s2mpu_np;
+	struct platform_device *s2mpu_pdev;
+
 	/*
 	 * Paired with smp_load_acquire(&s2mpu_notify),
 	 * Ensure memory stores hapenning during module init
 	 * are observed before executing the callback.
 	 */
+
+	/*
+	 * Called from pkvm_s2mpu's probe: the S2MPU platform device
+	 * exists NOW.  If the phy probed earlier and lost the race,
+	 * link it immediately instead of waiting for a
+	 * manual_control() call that may never come.  This runs in
+	 * probe context, where the driver-core lookup is safe.
+	 */
+	phy_drd = exynos_usbdrd_get_struct();
+	if (phy_drd && !phy_drd->s2mpu) {
+		s2mpu_np = of_parse_phandle(phy_drd->dev->of_node, "s2mpus", 0);
+		if (s2mpu_np) {
+			s2mpu_pdev = of_find_device_by_node(s2mpu_np);
+			of_node_put(s2mpu_np);
+			if (s2mpu_pdev)
+				phy_drd->s2mpu = &s2mpu_pdev->dev;
+		}
+	}
+
 	return cmpxchg_release(&s2mpu_notify, NULL, cb) ? -EBUSY : 0;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(exynos_usbdrd_set_s2mpu_pm_ops);
@@ -1859,7 +1885,6 @@ static int exynos_usbdrd_phy_power_off(struct phy *phy)
 int exynos_usbdrd_s2mpu_manual_control(bool on)
 {
 	struct exynos_usbdrd_phy *phy_drd;
-	int (*__s2mpu_notify)(struct device *dev, bool on);
 
 	pr_debug("%s s2mpu = %d\n", __func__, on);
 
@@ -1870,14 +1895,20 @@ int exynos_usbdrd_s2mpu_manual_control(bool on)
 		return -ENODEV;
 	}
 
-	/* Paired with cmpxchg_release in exynos_usbdrd_set_s2mpu_pm_ops. */
-	__s2mpu_notify = smp_load_acquire(&s2mpu_notify);
-	if (!phy_drd->s2mpu || !__s2mpu_notify)
+	if (!phy_drd->s2mpu)
 		return 0;
 
-	__s2mpu_notify(phy_drd->s2mpu, on);
+	/*
+	 * No device-tree lookups and no raw cross-module function
+	 * pointer here: this runs from the hsi0 power-domain path.
+	 * The link is resolved at probe time (phy probe, or pkvm's
+	 * probe via set_s2mpu_pm_ops above).  Control the S2MPU
+	 * through its driver's own runtime-PM implementation.
+	 */
+	if (on)
+		return pm_runtime_resume(phy_drd->s2mpu);
 
-	return 0;
+	return pm_runtime_suspend(phy_drd->s2mpu);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(exynos_usbdrd_s2mpu_manual_control);
 

